2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n**2-1. Business Understanding** (High understandability)\n1) Everpure sells all-flash enterprise storage arrays (FlashArray block; FlashBlade file/object) plus Purity software and Evergreen subscription models. 2) It is converting its installed base into recurring subscription revenue (ARR $2.0B, subscription services $1.7B in FY26) and expanding into \"Enterprise Data Cloud\" data management. 3) Growth is now amplified by AI/hyperscaler demand, including a confirmed direct-flash win at Meta [Actual \u2014 Forbes/blocksandfiles].\nUnderstandability: High.\n\n**2-2. Moat Score & Dynamics**\n| Moat type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction (widening/stable/narrowing) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets | 2 | Stable (NPS 84, Gartner Leader) |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Widening (RPO +41%, ActiveCluster/file mobility lock-in) |\n| Network effects | 1 | Stable (Pure1 community; not classic network effects) |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Widening in hyperscale direct-flash (40% power/cost advantage claims) [Estimate]; but Dell/NetApp buy NAND at larger scale |\n| Economies of scale | 1 | Narrowing vs Dell/NetApp/Huawei (they are larger) |\n| Regulatory barriers | 0 | \u2014 |\n| Data moat | 2 | Widening (Pure1 telemetry, AI Copilot, 1touch data intelligence) |\n| **Total** | **10/21** | **Narrow Moat, borderline wide \u2014 Direction: WIDENING** |\n\n**2-3. Lynch Classification: FAST GROWER with cyclical hardware component.** Checkpoints: revenue +16.2% FY26, +35% Q1-FY27 [Actual]; P/E 177\u00d7 \u2014 classic fast-grower trap territory; must monitor SBC dilution (fails the \"earnings quality\" checkpoint).\n\n**2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard** (FY26 unless noted) [Actual unless tagged]\n\u2022 Revenue growth: 3-yr CAGR +16.8% (pack 5-yr); FY26 +16%; Q1-FY27 +35% \u2014 **Excellent**\n\u2022 GAAP operating margin: 3.1% FY26; 1.9% Q1-FY27 \u2014 **Weak** (median 12-yr: \u221212.0%)\n\u2022 Non-GAAP operating margin: 17.3% FY26; guided 18.8% FY27 \u2014 **Good**\n\u2022 Net income: $188M FY26; $226M TTM \u2014 **Adequate**\n\u2022 FCF: $616M FY26; FCF/OCF conversion 70% [Actual] \u2014 **Good**\n\u2022 Adjusted FCF (OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC): **$134M** \u2014 **Concerning** (SBC $482M > NI $188M)\n\u2022 Net cash: cash+securities **$1.5B** [Actual, per press release] vs modest debt (~$0.3B incl. leases) [Estimate] \u2014 **Strong balance sheet**\n\u2022 ROIC: below WACC per independent source [Estimate, csimarket] \u2014 **Concerning**\nOverall: **Adequate-to-Good balance sheet; weak GAAP earnings quality; strong cash conversion headline, but SBC consumes 78% of FCF.**\n\n**2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u201315)**\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 (started FY26: $343M at avg ~$61 \u2014 good timing; but < SBC $482M)\n2. M&A: 2/3 (1touch fits platform, small; no impairment data)\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 (incremental ROIC > WACC in recent years; R&D-led model)\n4. Mix rationality: 2/3 (reinvesting in AI/hyperscaler capacity + buybacks; coherent)\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (Q1 beat, raised FY27 twice \u2014 strong credibility)\n+ Insider bonus: CEO/insider ownership 4.82% (~threshold, +0); recent 6-mo insider activity [Unconfirmed]; activist Jana Partners stake [Actual \u2014 Reuters 2026-06-30] = external pressure.\n**Total: 10/15 \u2014 Good.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550